issue_context.body:
While working on the .NET 7 vs .NET 7 NativeAOT micro benchmarks perf study, I've stumbled upon multiple micro benchmarks being 10x slower with NativeAOT on x64, despite having all available Instruction Sets enabled (it applies to arm64 as well, but I had no chance to verify it with Instruction Sets enabled yet).

Most of them are using `Vector<T>`. `Vector.IsHardwareAccelerated` returns `true`.

Sample app that exhibits the problem:

```cs
using System;
using System.Linq;
using System.Numerics;
using System.Runtime.Intrinsics.X86;

namespace aotVector
{
    internal class Program
    {
        static char[] _array = Enumerable.Repeat('a', 512).ToArray();
        static char[] _same = Enumerable.Repeat('a', 512).ToArray();

        static void Main()
        {
            Console.WriteLine(Avx2.IsSupported); // True
            Console.WriteLine(Vector.IsHardwareAccelerated); // True
            Console.WriteLine(Vector<ushort>.Count); // 16

            var left = new System.Span<char>(_array);
            var right = new System.Span<char>(_same);
            int result = 0;

            for (long i = 0; i < 10_000_000; i++)
                result ^= left.SequenceCompareTo(right);
            
            Console.WriteLine(result);
        }
    }
}
```

```xml
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net7.0</TargetFramework>
    <RuntimeIdentifier>win-x64</RuntimeIdentifier>
     <PlatformTarget>x64</PlatformTarget>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.DotNet.ILCompiler" Version="7.0.0-*" />
    <IlcArg Include="--instructionset:base,sse,sse2,sse3,sse4.1,sse4.2,avx,avx2,aes,bmi,bmi2,fma,lzcnt,pclmul,popcnt" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
```

